00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.840
It's a little on the long boy side.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.080
Here's our 6700 XT,
which is actually, okay,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.180
higher tier card than this
and it's about the same size,

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.870
which, again, would give you some idea

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.370
of what Intel was actually targeting.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.590
And you can see this in some
of the other specs as well.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.450
When we compared the raw pixel rate

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.150
and texture rate, memory
bandwidth, with something

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.219
like a 3060 Ti, the A770
kind of craps all over it.

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:29.640
But, because Intel is kind
of new to the driver game

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.004
when it comes to high
performance dedicated GPU's,

00:06:32.004 --> 00:06:37.004
it's speculated that aside from
any architectural oversights

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.420
there might have been
on a first gen product,

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.980
they have a lot to learn.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.377
Especially when it comes
to optimizing for older,

00:06:43.377 --> 00:06:46.860
especially DirectX 9 and earlier titles.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.660
And you can't not support them

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.626
because that's part of
the beauty of PC gaming

00:06:50.626 --> 00:06:54.660
is that you've got this
incredible back catalog

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.274
of games that you can go back and play,

00:06:56.274 --> 00:06:57.780
even on modern hardware.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.360
Though, sometimes does take
a little bit of hackery.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.970
In spite of all the delays,
one reassuringly modern thing

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.520
about this card is its feature set.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.380
Intel is claiming that
right out of the gate

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.900
they're going to have
Ray-tracing performance that is

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.860
up to 65 percent stronger,
peak, compared to their rivals.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.260
Okay, so up to, peak.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:17.760
That's a lot of qualifications, but,

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.350
hey, at least they're
gonna support ray-tracing

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.990
right outta the gate and they're

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.610
gonna have support for Xe Super Sampling.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.530
XESS, which is a competitor to

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.330
NVIDIA's Deep Learning Super Sampling

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:30.210
and AMD's Fidelity
Effects Super Resolution.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:33.537
Using AI to boost frame
rates at higher resolutions.

00:07:33.537 --> 00:07:37.290
How well it works though,
remains to be seen

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.940
and based on how much work NVIDIA and AMD

00:07:40.818 --> 00:07:41.651
have already put into this technology

00:07:41.651 --> 00:07:44.448
I would say Intel is going
to be playing catch up.

00:07:44.448 --> 00:07:46.590
Where they won't be playing catch up

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.804
is in their display interfaces.

00:07:47.804 --> 00:07:51.480
On the back here, we're
going to find HDMI 2.1.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.380
So you'll be able to do up to 4K 120 Hertz

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.433
or 8K 60 I believe, you
can anything on 2.1.

00:07:58.433 --> 00:08:01.110
And this is where things get really cool.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:05.332
This is our first hands
on with display Port 2.0.

00:08:05.332 --> 00:08:09.170
However, unlike previous
version of display Port,

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:13.830
DP 2.0 is not just a cut and
dried, here's what you get.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.010
It's divided up into ultra high bit rate,

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:22.010
UHBR 10, 13.5 I think, and 20.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.270
And what those numbers mean
is the data rate times four.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:32.270
So these are DP 2.0 UHBR 10

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.704
for a total of 40
gigabit per second ports.

00:08:36.704 --> 00:08:38.790
Okay. It's a little confusing.

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:41.670
Kind of wish it was just UHBR 40

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:44.790
so that that would be a little
more intuitive for normies.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.440
But, in a world where USB naming

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:48.811
conventions exist I guess this is a win.

00:08:48.811 --> 00:08:50.010
(chuckling)

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.140
Another win, and another modern feature

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.390
is Intel Support for AV1 and coding.

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:57.900
And, not on this GPU, but we
have actually played around

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.490
with it a fair bit in the last week

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.490
on Intel ARC on the A380 and
it's pretty freaking cool.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.359
At 3500 kilobit per second,

00:09:07.359 --> 00:09:11.520
our encoded game streaming
footage looks about the same

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.410
as NVIDIA's end bank at
6,000 kilobit per second.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.020
So what this means is that
once platforms like Twitch

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.780
support it, you can expect
to be able to stream

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.948
at the same quality that people can today,

00:09:23.948 --> 00:09:26.520
on a much lesser internet connection

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.725
or you can expect to see
better quality Twitch streams.

00:09:29.725 --> 00:09:31.380
Pretty cool.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.090
For all my positivity, though.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:36.090
And as much as I would love
for there to be a valid

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.957
third competitor in the
dedicated graphics card space,

00:09:38.957 --> 00:09:42.390
I do have to temper my
enthusiasm a little bit

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.270
because Intel is up
against a lot of obstacles.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.580
They've gotta figure out
that backwards compatibility

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.950
game performance, they've
gotta compete with

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:54.270
AMD and NVIDIA both releasing
new series of GPU's when

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:55.980
ARC Alchemist was really supposed to be

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.764
competing with the last series of GPU's.

00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:01.980
And another big one is that we talk about

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:05.245
second hand GPU's a lot and
given the recent crypto crash

00:10:05.245 --> 00:10:08.910
there's a lot of them out there
at very aggressive pricing.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:11.344
And the reality of it is that
Intel doesn't have any used

00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:15.450
GPU's and they certainly don't
benefit from selling them.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.910
So it's going to be an
extremely competitive market.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.430
They seem pretty confident
in their 329 pricing

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.440
